<section class="aim" id="aim">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="kicker">01 — the single aim</div>
    <div class="aim-statement">We exist to gather everyone who wants Scotland to leave the union. Nothing else.</div>
    <p class="aim-note">No housing policy. No tax policy. No leader to fall out over. The single aim is what makes this a big tent instead of another party — you can disagree with every other new radical about everything except this one thing, and still belong.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sovereignty" id="sovereignty">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="kicker">02 — the sovereign prerequisite</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">We are not asking permission. We're recognising a fact.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sov-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Joining as a new radical means accepting one premise: sovereignty in Scotland rests with the Scottish people, not with any parliament. That's not a slogan — it's a status affirmed in the <strong>Claim of Right</strong>, and it doesn't expire and can't be revoked by a vote at Westminster.</p>
        <p>Because that right already exists, the network doesn't campaign to be granted independence. It campaigns as people who already possess the right to decide their own constitutional future, and who are simply organising to exercise it.</p>
        <p>That's the whole prerequisite. Everything else — how you'd vote in a second independence referendum on any other question, which party you support, what an independent Scotland should look like — is yours to decide. The network takes no position on any of it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="member-card">
        <div class="card-top">
          <svg class="card-cap" viewBox="0 0 40 46" fill="none"><path d="M20 2C9 8 4 18 6 30c1 6 6 10 14 10s13-4 14-10c2-12-3-22-14-28z" fill="#0067bf"/><rect x="3" y="40" width="34" height="4" rx="1" fill="#0067bf"/></svg>
          <div class="card-no">member<strong>0001</strong></div>
        </div>
        <p class="card-quote">"I am a new radical. I assert the Scottish people's inalienable right to complete freedom, the exercise of our sovereignty and the integrity of our national territory."</p>
        <p class="card-quote2">By the authority of our constitutional tradition enshrined in the Declaration of Arbroath 1320 and the active authority of the Claim of Right 1689, our sovereignty is permanent. Therefore we need not ask for our freedom; we simply claim it, and declare that Scotland's connection to the union is, and of right ought to be, totally dissolved.</p>
        <div class="card-foot">the scottish new radicals · membership card</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-teaser" id="case-teaser">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="kicker">03 — the constitutional case</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Was the Union ever lawfully consented to?</h2>
    <p class="lede">On 25 March 1707, the Scottish Parliament adjourned — not dissolved — to 22 April, opening a window in which the Convention of Estates, the only body entitled to approve or reject a transfer of sovereignty, could have been summoned. Knowing this, England made sure that session never took place. The "Union," it turned out, was nothing other than the annexation of Scotland.</p>
    <div class="teaser-quote">
      No adjourned sitting. No Convention of Estates. No tested consent.
      <span>— the full sequence, the primary sources, and why it was suppressed on three separate levels</span>
    </div>
    <div class="teaser-cta">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/the-case/">read the full case →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="history" id="history">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="kicker">04 — the historic hook</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">The same struggle, two centuries apart</h2>
      <p class="lede">The name isn't a marketing choice. It's a claim of lineage.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hist-grid">
      <div class="hist-col">
        <div class="hist-year">1820</div>
        <h3>the radicals</h3>
        <p>Weavers, artisans and tradesmen across the west of Scotland struck for democratic rights and self-government, in a movement that ended in trial and execution but became a founding memory of Scottish working-class politics.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hist-col">
        <div class="hist-year">today</div>
        <h3>new radicals</h3>
        <p>No party machine, no funded office, no professional class of organisers — the same low-cost, high-conviction shape as 1820, rebuilt for a network that can be run from a shared inbox and a printer's tab.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="how" id="join-explainer">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="kicker">05 — how joining works</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Three steps, no admin team</h2>
      <p class="lede">The whole system runs without a bank account, because nothing about it requires one.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">step one</div>
        <h3>sign up online</h3>
        <p>Add your name to the list below. That's the only thing that happens on the website — no payment, no account, no data beyond what's needed to send you a card.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">step two</div>
        <h3>send an SAE</h3>
        <p>Post a stamped-addressed envelope to the holding address you're sent. You cover the postage — it's the only cost of belonging, and it's what keeps the network free to run.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">step three</div>
        <h3>get your number</h3>
        <p>A sequentially numbered card comes back in your own envelope — Member 0001 through Member x000 — printed on credit paid for by donors, drawn down one batch at a time.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

